+## Execution evidence
+
+Each successful response adds `execution_evidence`, with the fixed contract's exact schema/profile, requested backend, prompt length, one decode, reset flags, and observed FA/classifier/lookup node records. Full-attention count comes from the typed boolean `qwen35.attention.recurrent_layers` array, whose length must equal block count and contain at least one full layer. Production has 16 full layers; synthetic fixtures have one or two.
+
+Evidence requires BOTH the node's `GGML_TENSOR_FLAG_COMPUTE` and the public scheduler callback's successful synchronized post-execution (`ask=false`) phase. This matters because the pinned scheduler also visits inactive mixed-input select branches. Pre-execution callbacks and inactive placeholders never count. Callback exceptions never cross the C boundary; failures are remembered and the request is rejected after cleanup, rather than stopping later graph work silently.
+
+Device records are taken from actual tensor allocation/buffer types and registered backend devices, not request flags. In this pin, CPU and CPU_Mapped buffer types have null device pointers; the implementation identifies those exact public buffer-type identities (including a zero-size `ggml_backend_cpu_buffer_from_ptr` probe) and resolves the registered CPU device. It never infers CPU from a buffer-name prefix. CUDA pinned/host buffers cannot serve as GPU execution evidence.
+
+Admission requires the exact unique FA-node count, one classifier matmul, one actually computed token lookup, no computed token-weight vocabulary matmul, and CPU token weights. CUDA additionally requires CUDA GPU/IGPU FA outputs and classifier output/weight allocations. CPU reports CPU honestly. This is synchronized scheduler/allocation evidence, not a profiler kernel trace or a guarantee of no unused allocation.
