-1. Analytically known identity-residual last-position projection, exactly 255 ordered raw values, no softmax or vector normalization.
-2. A/B/A repeated-prompt equality across another prompt and malformed requests, demonstrating recurrent/KV reset.
-3. Empty/long/multimodal/mismatched inputs rejected and a following valid request succeeds.
-4. Missing/wrong/unsupported metadata rejected before model execution.
-5. Unit protocol coverage, including integer bounds, duplicate fields, excessive nesting, and malformed JSON; CLI errors and invalid GGUF rejection.
-6. The deeper grouped-GDN model matches a separate noncausal control bit-for-bit, while a deliberately causal control differs on multi-token prompts. A one-token negative control agrees; A/B/A resets remain exact after a full-capacity 64-token whole-prompt batch. The early full-attention layer influences later layers, so this test can distinguish noncausal semantics at the final token.
-7. Source verification rejects changed files despite an unchanged pinned marker, extra native files, missing files, and redirected source symlinks.
-
-The glide-test-attention-control executable is a separate **test-only** harness. It accepts only files explicitly marked test.synthetic_fixture=true. It is not installed or delivered as the production runner; the production executable still rejects causal model metadata and cannot enable causal attention through its CLI.
-
-The analytic CPU reference rounds the hidden activation to BF16 before the BF16-head dot product, matching upstream CPU `vec_dot_type=BF16`. Backend numerical tolerances, actual GLiDE prediction parity, task quality, and performance still require real-weight CPU/GPU validation. Synthetic metadata intentionally uses the pinned identity strings to test the loader contract; it is not evidence of genuine GLiDE weights or tokenization.
